@* A search or filter text field with an × that clears it and leaves the caret in the field (#503).

   Shared rather than copied, and this is the case that proves why: MudBlazor's `Clearable` gives the × for
   free, so four fields could each have grown one — and every one of them would have had the same defect, that
   clearing loses the caret. The user then clicks ×, and has to click the field again before typing, which is a
   one-click action costing three. Fixed once here, it cannot land on four fields and miss the fifth.

   Esc is the keyboard equivalent and clears only when there is text, so an empty field lets it through to
   whatever else Esc means there (ADR 0550). The desktop is canonical and does exactly the same (ADR 0511).

   The × is MudBlazor's own adornment, so it appears whenever there is text and is not hover-gated — touch has
   no hover (ADR 0491). *@

<MudTextField @ref="_field" T="string" Value="@Value" ValueChanged="OnValueChangedAsync"
              Immediate="@Immediate" Clearable="true" OnClearButtonClick="ClearAsync" OnKeyDown="OnKeyDownAsync"
              Label="@Label" Placeholder="@Placeholder" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Margin="Margin.Dense"
              Adornment="@Adornment" AdornmentIcon="@AdornmentIcon"
              Style="@Style" Class="@Class" />

@code {
    private MudTextField<string>? _field;

    /// <summary>The text in the field. Two-way: use <c>@bind-Value</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Value { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string?> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised after the field is emptied by the × or by Esc, for a caller that re-runs a query.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback Cleared { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised for every key, so a caller can still act on Enter. Esc is consumed here.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KeyboardEventArgs> OnKeyDown { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The floating label, where the field sits in a row of labelled controls.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Label { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Placeholder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>False (the default) commits on blur, which is MudBlazor's behaviour for a plain filter field.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Immediate { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public Adornment Adornment { get; set; } = Adornment.None;

    [Parameter]
    public string? AdornmentIcon { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Style { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }

    // Normalised to the empty string, never null. MudBlazor's own clear sets the value to `default`, so every
    // caller would otherwise have to be null-safe on a path that only happens when the × is clicked — and the
    // one that is not would hand null to JS interop and take the pane down.
    private async Task OnValueChangedAsync(string? value)
    {
        Value = value ?? string.Empty;
        await ValueChanged.InvokeAsync(Value);
    }

    private async Task OnKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs args)
    {
        // Only when there is something to clear: an empty field must not swallow Esc from whatever else wants it.
        if (args.Key == "Escape" && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(Value))
        {
            await ClearAsync();
            return;
        }

        await OnKeyDown.InvokeAsync(args);
    }

    // The order matters: empty the value first, THEN focus. Focusing a field whose value is still the old one
    // and letting the clear land afterwards is how a cleared field re-commits what the user just removed.
    private async Task ClearAsync()
    {
        await OnValueChangedAsync(string.Empty);
        await Cleared.InvokeAsync();

        if (_field is not null)
        {
            await _field.FocusAsync();
        }
    }
}
